'''Sant Dídac''' ({{Literal translation|{{W|Didacus of Alcalá|Saint Didacus}}}}; {{Langx|mxi|سَانْ دِيدَاكُوسْ|San Didákus}}) is a [[Communes of Balisca|barrio]] and major {{W|business district}} located in the [[Expansión]] borough of [[Castejón]]. It is one of Castejón's four business districts, along with [[Ampurias]]' [[Mercadero]], [[Gistaín]] in [[Charxes]] and [[Trinacria]]'s [[Basilio]], across [[Salto Bay]]. 

Sant Dídac's [[Seventh Avenue (Castejón)|Avenida Séptimo]] contains several of [[List of tallest buildings in Castejón|Castejón]]'s and [[List of tallest buildings in Balisca|Balisca's]] tallest skyscrapeΩrs, and is increasingly known for the recent proliferation of [[W:Pencil tower|pencil towers]]. About three-quarters of the jobs in Sant Dídac are in the tech, {{W|biotech}}, financial, professional, and associated business services sectors.

Sant Dídac's [[W:Real estate|real estate]] prices are some of the most expensive in Latin America, with the value of the district, including real estate, estimated to exceed US$2 trillion in 2020; median residential property sale prices in Expansión approximated US$1,300 per square foot ($13,800/m2) as of 2024, while Sant Dídac claimed the highest retail rents in the city, at US$2,400 per square foot ($26,000/m2) per year in 2024. In the 21st century it has emerged as the tertiary [[W:Central business district|business district]] of Castejón, and is an area where many [[W:Multinational corporations|multinational]] financial services corporations have their headquarters. 

The district forms the northernmost extremity of the ''[[Aixo Sol]]'' of [[Castejón]], which radiates to the northwest and southeast from the [[Arco Merca]]. The district lies between the [[Alagón (river)|Alagón]] river and [[Oñoico Bay]]. 

Sant Dídac is considered a prime area for the Metropolitan and borough administration for redevelopment under the [[Casajuan Plan]]. It is also an integrated response to encourage the conversion of office space vacated by domestic and multinational companies during the {{W|COVID-19 pandemic}} into residential and {{W|mixed use development}}.

== History ==

===Rioting during 2021 unrest===
The barrio was the site of mass demonstrations over increases in [[W:Economic inequality|income inequality]], [[W:Social stratification|social stratification]] and an increasing lack of [[W:Affordable housing|affordable housing]] for non-nationals, concurrent with [[2021 Baliscan civil unrest|protests in other cities in Balisca]] in the summer of 2021. By July 2021, many of the protests, which had been held every day since 28 June, drew more than 10,000 participants. Protests continued into August, September, and October 2021, often drawing thousands.

As of August 2021, the majority of the protests in Castejón were peaceful, but an increasing number of demonstrations involved rioting, heated confrontations with the [[Mozos de Escuadra]] and counter-protesters, and use of {{W|molotovs}} and other [[W:Improvised weapon|improvised weapons]]. On 3 September, the situation escalated as a group of people began carrying out vandalism; seizing, looting, and burning down many {{W|banks}}, {{W|luxury business}}es and disabling {{W|electric signage}}, along with extensive infrastructure damage. Eighty-one stores sustained major damage, including seventeen completely destroyed. On the same day, at the request of the communal council, the [[Metropolitan Council of Castejón|Metropolitan Council]] adopted a {{W|state of emergency}}, authorizing the deployment of the Mozos de Escuadra across the main boroughs in an attempt prevent the destruction of communal infrastructure. A curfew was declared on 6 September in the entirety of the Expansión area. There continued to be well documented instances of arson, looting, vandalism, and injuries.

As a result of the widespread dissatisfaction among residents with the response to the unrest, the barrio [[2021 Castejón regional election|voted out]] every incumbent member of it's delegation to the [[Metropolitan Council of Castejón]], and delivered a new delegation with a supermajority in favor of adopting a new [[communal charter]] for the first time since 1973. The commune would implement six new additions to the social contract; raising the minimum proportion of {{W|affordable housing}} from 20 (the communal minimum) to 40%, minimums for {{W|student housing}}, eliminating {{w|parking minimums}} and establishing tighter {{W|rent controls}}. 

